findings.body:
**Concern.** `knownBinarySize` judges the blob, which is the bytes stored in git. A loader reads the checked-out file. The built-in `working-tree-encoding` attribute in `.gitattributes` rewrites those bytes on checkout. It needs no config driver, so ADR 0050's "the attributes half alone is inert" does not cover it. Nothing in `src/` consults attributes when judging a binary:

```ts
export function knownBinarySize(blob: Buffer, formats: BinaryFormat[]): number | false {
  if (!holdsNul(blob)) return false;
  if (holdsZipRecord(blob) || holdsPharStub(blob) || opensWithTarHeader(blob)) return false;
  return opensWithMagic(blob, formats) && blob.length;
}
```

On checkout, git's `encode_to_worktree` converts the blob from UTF-8 into the named encoding. It skips the conversion only when the blob is not valid UTF-8, and it does no binary or NUL check first. `IBM037` (EBCDIC) maps all 256 byte values, so a valid UTF-8 blob can be written out as any bytes at all. For example, the output `PK\x05\x06` comes from the characters `&.\t\u0086`, so the blob never contains the zip signature literally. Four of the seven magics are themselves valid UTF-8: ICO `\0\0\x01\0`, TIFF, Matroska and TTF. IBM037 also maps ICO's magic bytes to themselves.

**Trigger.**
1. Commit a one-line `.gitattributes`: `assets/x.ico working-tree-encoding=IBM037`.
2. Commit `assets/x.ico`, mode 100644 and at most 1 MiB. It is `\0\0\x01\0` followed by the UTF-8 preimage of a deflated zip of Python. It holds a NUL and opens with the ICO magic. It carries no literal `PK\x05\x06`, `PK\x03\x04`, `__halt_compiler` or tar header. It is skipped at 0 lines and not added to `forged`.
3. Add one counted line: `sys.path.insert(0, 'assets/x.ico')`.

**Consequence.** Every checkout after the merge writes a real zip, which zipimport loads past the 4-byte prefix (`phar://` works the same way). This brings back the hole ADR 0154 closes by refusing a zip end record "anywhere": megabytes of compressed source behind a ≤1 MiB blob, and about four such files per 4 MiB budget. The `.gitattributes` line counts as 1 source line and raises no flag. This is not a regression, since before this PR any NUL-holding blob was skipped. It is a working bypass of a refusal this PR adds and documents.

**Fix direction.** Git re-encodes only a blob that decodes as UTF-8. One fix is to refuse any blob that is entirely valid UTF-8: `if (!holdsNul(blob) || isUtf8(blob)) return false;` with `import { isUtf8 } from 'node:buffer'`. Real ICO, TIFF, MKV and TTF data almost never decodes as UTF-8. The other fix is to read `git check-attr --source=<branch> working-tree-encoding` for each side and refuse the skip when it is set.

